The translation lives in two files: [38;5;10mtranslation/Cargo.toml[0m and [38;5;10mtranslation/src/main.rs[0m. Structure mirrors the C one-to-one ([38;5;10mHouse[0m, [38;5;10madd_floor[0m, [38;5;10madd_bedrooms[0m, [38;5;10mprint_house[0m, [38;5;10mrun[0m, [38;5;10mparse_val[0m, [38;5;10mmain[0m), with three places needing care:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mfgets[0m semantics — reads at most 99 bytes, stops [3mafter[23m a newline and keeps it, never reads past end of line. On immediate EOF the buffer stays empty, which then fails parsing and prints [38;5;10mAn error occurred[0m. I read stdin a byte at a time rather than using [38;5;10mread_line[0m so nothing beyond the line is consumed.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics — hand-rolled base-10 parser rather than Rust's [38;5;10mparse::<i32>()[0m, because the C accepts trailing garbage ([38;5;10m42abc[0m → 42, [38;5;10m0x10[0m → 0) and rejects only when zero characters were consumed. It also emulates 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m with [38;5;10mERANGE[0m: [38;5;10m2147483648[0m fails the [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m range check while [38;5;10m99999999999999999999999[0m fails via the errno check, and both paths land on the same error message. [38;5;10mparse_val[0m keeps the C's original check order ([38;5;10mendp != str[0m, then [38;5;10merrno[0m, then range).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Signed overflow — [38;5;10mbedrooms += extra_bedrooms[0m is UB in C but wraps in practice, so I used [38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m. This matters because [38;5;10mrun[0m is called twice on the same mutated house, so a large input like [38;5;10m2147483640[0m wraps around; verified identical.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Bathrooms only ever take values 2.5/3.5/4.5, so [38;5;10m{:.1}[0m and [38;5;10m%.1f[0m agree exactly with no rounding-mode ambiguity. I did not modify anything in [38;5;10mc_src/[0m; the C binary I compiled for comparison went to a temp dir and has been cleaned up.[0m[0m
